{
  "id": "COMPOUND:29888 - PROTEIN:6138",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a complex network of interactions involving 2-Methylheptyl propanoate, which is linked to a sialyltransferase enzyme, and this enzyme is in turn targeted by multiple compounds, including CMP-N-acetylneuraminate-beta-1,4-galactoside alpha-2,3-sialyltransferase and 6'-O-Z-Para-Methoxycinnamoylharpagide. These compounds are all implicated in targeting the 26S proteasome non-ATPase regulatory subunit 14, indicating a potential pathway where 2-Methylheptyl propanoate may influence the activity of this proteasome subunit through its interaction with these enzymes. The evidence suggests that 2-Methylheptyl propanoate may act as a modulator or activator of the sialyltransferase, which in turn affects the proteasome subunit, though the exact mechanism of this interaction remains unclear. The presence of multiple compounds targeting the same subunit supports the idea of a broader biological role for 2-Methylheptyl propanoate in regulating proteasome function, though further investigation is needed to elucidate the precise molecular mechanism.",
